## Feedback-channel boundary

FB-* denotes a logical information dependency that updates a process-model belief.
It is not evidence of a network socket, packet stream, message bus,
database connection, or attacker-accessible transport. Realize the update
through a declared AI surface—prompt/context input, retrieved content, tool result,
memory state, agent message, or model output—unless the supplied
architecture explicitly identifies an accessible transport.

When exact reachable-capability evidence supports active access, describe how
the attacker changes a declared AI surface. Without that evidence, explain the
structural condition instead.

Examples of declared AI-surface realizations are prompt/context injection,
retrieved-content poisoning, tool-result fabrication, memory poisoning,
agent-message tampering, and model-output manipulation. Use one only when the
scenario evidence names that surface and supports access to it.

Never infer packet interception, man-in-the-middle access (MITM), network delay,
traffic blocking, network-signal spoofing, communication-link severing,
credential theft, account takeover, session hijacking or fixation
(session hijacking/fixation), or generic flooding or denial of service
(generic flooding/DoS) from the word “channel.” Active manipulation
requires exact supplied access evidence; otherwise describe the structural
condition. No reachable capabilities means no invented interface or privilege.
